From c26c43bc92e2d770bfc4674affb5dcac7d4d5589 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Sat, 17 Oct 2026 04:13:55 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 169/169] Kernel preparation: a step's Triton kernels compile
 with the runner's JIT warmup

The V2 runner's step kernels compiled on first launch, during memory
profiling's dummy runs and the warm-up steps: input preparation, block tables,
staged writes and KV block zeroing, the sampler and its sampling parameters,
logprobs, grammar masks, rejection sampling, greedy verification from
vocabulary shards, DSpark's vocabulary-parallel greedy drafts and input
preparation, DS4.1's CED row packing and Engram lookback window. Each now has a
warmup launcher (triton_kernel_dispatcher_with_warmup around the native kernel,
so every launch is the same kernel[grid](...) call) whose warmup inputs list
the signatures this configuration can launch, registered while the runner's
registry collects: the model state's construction and a new scope after the
samplers are built in load_model. They compile in _prepare_kernels, before
any distributed work.

Variants come from the runner's buffers, the loaded LM head (head dtype,
padded vocabulary shards), the scheduler's sizes and the KV cache groups the
spec gives, derived before the KV cache exists with the same width and
kernel-block-size rules initialize_kv_cache uses (select_kernel_block_sizes
mirrors init_attn_backend's selection without building metadata). CEDState
registers its packings instead of launching them, adding the padding-mask
gather and keeping only the scatters the model makes.
